== Limitations
The project has succesfully produced a harmonized @DTM of the Rhine catchment area throughout the past nine weeks. Furthermore, the automated pipeline for processing and analysis has been completed and tested, resulting in the evaluation of between bordering regions to determine the quality of the product.

However, despite these successes there are limitations to the achievements of the project. The first of which is the scope of the project itself. While not origionally intended, the project group consisted of only three, instead of six, students for a period of ten weeks. Within this timeframe, the aquisition, harmonisation, border analysis, evaluation, project management, and reporting had to be completed. As discussed during meetings and feedback opertunities during the project, the efforts of all three members were required at the same time for each of these topics. For example, the harmonisation in week 5 came to a complete stop due to the required reporting for that week. As a result, several initially intended parts of the scope were cut, including point cloud @DTM reextraction, automated artifact correction, and more extensive validation of the catchment area @DTM.

The available time and researches were further reduced by previously identified risks (@Risk_Assessment_Table). During week six the processing server was unavailable due to unannounced maintenance. During this time, coding and documentation continued, but no testing or processing was possible, loosing us precious time. Furthermore, personal circumstances affected the availability of two team members, one during week 8 and the other during week 9. These events occured during the final stages of analysis and reporting, leaving little time for adressing final bug fixes. However, through the specified mitigation, the impact was minimized as best as possible.

A secondary limitation is the reliance on external datasets. The quality of the end product is determined by the sum of its parts. Throughout the analysis it became clear that most artifacts are the result of temporal, spatial and processing differences between datasets, and not neccesarily the processing pipeline. In several cases, large artifacts such as extrapolation data, or misaligned tiles caused large disagreements in the elevation data. In other cases, the exact cause could not be estabilished due to the unavailability of data about processing methodology, or point cloud data to evaluate the raw data for clues.

Another limitation is that the scope of the project was reduced to focus primarily on @DTM:pls instead of point clouds. While it significantly reduced the total workload, it did prevent evaluating raw instead of processed elevation data. The raw data should in theory contain fewer processing differences such as ground filtering or water handling. Furthermore, using one unified processing method would have eliminated the need for a water mask.

The water mask itself also introduces limitations. While not an origionally intended element of the pipeline, the analysis proved that water was a consistent source of large disagreements and would need to be filtered out for any meaningful analysis to be carried out. However, due to the impromptu nature of this feature, it makes assumptions about water such as having small elevation changes, and requires a course, incomplete existing mask to function. So while the analysis benefitted greatly from the feature, it will require more time and effort to perfect, should this project be continued.

Several limitations pertaining to the border analysis itself also exist. 